---
description: automated testing strategy (E2E, integration L1/L2, unit — what to test and how)
alwaysApply: false
---
Apply when writing or changing automated tests. Pair with stack tooling (`frontend-testing`, go-core / rust-core / backend stack rules) and `monorepo-architecture` when E2E hits in-repo backends. CI wiring for when tests run: `ci-github`. Production code should follow code-craft Composition so unit-testable leaves exist. Greenfield always installs this file (see `bootstrap-select-rules`).

### Greenfield (day one)

- Every new project scaffold **must** include a working unit-test runner for each stack present and **at least one minimal passing test**.
- Run the documented test command once during scaffold and confirm it is green before calling bootstrap done.
- A trivial smoke test is enough at birth (e.g. `assert 1 + 1 == 2`, `expect(true).toBe(true)`, `TestTrue`, empty `#[test] fn smoke()`). The goal is a harness ready to extend — not full coverage on day one.
- E2E/Playwright and rich L2 suites may come later; do not block greenfield on them. When React is present, still install `frontend-testing` and prefer a minimal unit (**Vitest** + RTL on Vite; Jest only if not Vite) green test on day one; add Playwright when E2E work starts.
- Document the exact local test command on the root README (see `documentation.mdc`).

### Local runs

- Developers must be able to run the same test levels **locally from the terminal** (not only in CI): unit, L2, and E2E/L1 as applicable.
- Prefer documented package scripts or standard toolchain commands (e.g. `npm test`, `npx playwright test`, `go test ./...`, `pytest`, `cargo test`) and make them discoverable from the project README (brief commands on the README; longer notes may live in `docs/` per `documentation.mdc`).
- CI workflows should invoke those same scripts/commands so local and CI stay aligned.
- Monorepo E2E locally: bring up the test stack (compose/seed) as documented, then run Playwright — same isolated data rules as CI.

### Principles (all levels)

- Assert **observable behavior and contracts**, never implementation details (DOM tag names, private helpers, call order of internals, “uses X then Y internally”).
- Prefer fewer, higher-signal tests over exhaustive low-value ones.
- Prefer a real isolated test environment over mocks when first-party systems can be isolated. Use L1 mocks only for **third parties you cannot isolate** (e.g. email/payments without a usable sandbox API).

### E2E (full system)

- Cover **main user journeys** (highest-value requirements) through the real UI.
- Tooling: Playwright (see `frontend-testing`).
- Monorepo: run against **real backends** in a dedicated test environment with **isolated seeded data** (see `monorepo-architecture`) — never against day-to-day developer data.
- Frontend-only: same Playwright flows; no backend/seed concern.
- If a main path depends on non-isolatable third parties, cover it with **L1** (or L2 if the UI is the wrong surface), not a fragile full E2E.

### Integration L1 (Playwright + selective mocks)

- Same UI driver as E2E; **mock only** externals that cannot live in the test env.
- Use for important paths that need the UI but cannot be fully real end-to-end.
- Prefer L1 for a **subset** of side paths when full E2E for every side path would blow CI time and L2 alone would lose too much confidence.

### Integration L2 (no UI — backend / workers)

- Use when the behavior is not a UI journey (workers, schedulers, pure API contracts) or when many **side paths** would make Playwright-only coverage too slow for CI.
- Cover **component/API contracts**: happy paths plus edge cases (errors, filters, authz failures, empty sets) so callers’ expectations are not silently broken.
- Includes overall behavior of **orchestrating** service/usecase entrypoints (multi-step workflows).
- Tooling: stack-standard libraries (see go-core, rust-core, Python FastAPI, Go/Rust stack testing sections).

### Unit

- Purpose: lock the **contract of a single-job function** so a change that breaks other callers fails fast and points at that unit (granularity when a large E2E fails).
- **Unit-test leaves, not orchestrators.** Do **not** unit-test feature/usecase service entrypoints that only sequence many steps (update DB + log + notify + …). Cover that composed behavior with E2E or L2.
- **Do** unit-test the single-job helpers those entrypoints call. If a helper still does multiple jobs, split again and unit-test the new leaves — same idea all the way down (code-craft Composition).
- Test **what** a leaf returns/does for given inputs — not **how**.
- Skip trivial pass-throughs. If you need heavy mocking of collaborators to unit-test something, you are usually testing an orchestrator or the wrong layer — move coverage up (L2/E2E) or extract a real leaf.

### Choosing a level

1. Main user path → E2E (or L1 if a third party blocks).
2. Important non-main / side path → L1 subset if UI-valuable and CI budget allows; else L2.
3. Contracts/edge cases between backend components, workers, endpoints → L2 (includes orchestrators).
4. Single-job leaves with clear contracts → unit (not the multi-step callers).
